-- Schema v1 → v2: per-profile cube names.
--
-- Until v1 a cube's display name lived on the `cubes` row, which is
-- keyed by MAC and therefore shared by every profile that pairs the same
-- physical cube. Renaming a cube under one profile changed it for all of
-- them. `cube_names` splits the user's label away from the hardware
-- record; see CubeNames.sq for the reasoning.
--
-- This is the app's first migration file. The `.sq` files always describe
-- the *current* schema; each `.sqm` is a delta applied to installs that
-- are behind, and `QbsmarterDatabase.Schema.version` is the highest
-- migration number plus one — so this file alone puts the schema at
-- version 2. Installs created before it existed report user_version = 1
-- and run it; fresh installs are created straight from the .sq files at
-- the current version and never see it.
--
-- **One change per file, and a shipped file is frozen.** The version a
-- device reports is decided by the *set* of migration files, not by what
-- is inside them. Extending a file that has already reached a device
-- strands that device silently: it is already stamped with the version
-- the file produces, so it will never run the part that was added later.
-- Every schema change since has its own `.sqm` for exactly that reason.

CREATE TABLE cube_names (
    user_id TEXT NOT NULL REFERENCES users(id) ON DELETE CASCADE,
    mac TEXT NOT NULL,
    name TEXT NOT NULL,
    PRIMARY KEY (user_id, mac)
);

-- Carry every existing name across as an override belonging to the
-- profile that currently owns the cube. Whoever renamed a cube keeps
-- seeing their name; nobody else inherits it.
--
-- Cubes whose owning profile no longer exists are skipped. Those rows are
-- the wreckage of profile deletions made while foreign keys were
-- unenforced (swept in `2.sqm`), and there is no profile for the name to
-- belong to. Filtering here rather than relying on a cleanup that runs
-- later also makes this file correct under foreign-key enforcement on its
-- own — which matters, because it will be run long after it was written,
-- on databases that arrive at it by routes nobody has thought about yet.
--
-- `cubes.name` is left as it is rather than being cleared. Post-migration
-- it means "the name the cube advertises", and for a renamed cube it
-- briefly holds the user's label instead — harmless, because the owning
-- profile now reads its name from cube_names, and the next connect
-- overwrites the column with the real advertised name (`cubes.upsert`
-- assigns it whenever the cube reports one). Clearing it here would be
-- worse: it would blank the fallback name for every cube that is never
-- connected again.
INSERT INTO cube_names (user_id, mac, name)
SELECT user_id, mac, name FROM cubes
WHERE name IS NOT NULL AND user_id IN (SELECT id FROM users);
